+# US-11: Unit tests for core services
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Epic | Quality / Test Plan section 5 |
+| Requirement | Test Plan 5 (unit testing); supports MLE-F-007, MLE-F-008, MLE-F-009 |
+| Points / sprint | 5 / Sprint 1 |
+| Owner | Varnika (Member 2) |
+
+**Story.** As a developer I want each core service tested on its own, so that a failure points at the broken service and not at the page in front of it.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+- [x] Catalogue, parameter validation, request validation, data pipeline, artifact storage, dataset locator, job runner and the run model each have direct unit tests.
+- [x] Boundary values are tested on both sides (just inside, just outside) for every numeric range.
+- [x] Failure paths are tested, not only the happy path.
+- [x] Every test is tagged with the requirement it covers (`req("MLE-F-...")`), so the Requirement Traceability Matrix can be filled from the test run.
+- [x] Statement coverage of the `training` package is 100% (target in the Test Plan: at least 80%).
+- [x] Tests need no network, no real dataset and no leftover files; each test gets its own temp folders.
+
+## Result
+
+**139 tests pass, 100% statement coverage of `training/`.**
+
+Run from the repository root:
+
+```
+python -I -m pytest -c member2_dev/pytest.ini -p no:cacheprovider --cov=training --cov-report=term-missing
+```
+
+## Defect found by these tests
+
+`test_bad_test_sizes[nan]` showed that a JSON body with `"test_size": NaN` (or `Infinity`), which Python's JSON parser accepts, crashed `_valid_test_size` and would have returned a 500 error. Fixed with a finiteness check in `training/schemas.py`; regression tests added at unit and API level.
+
+## Files
+
+| File | Purpose |
+| --- | --- |
+| `training/tests/test_services_unit.py` | Direct tests of schema, locator, artifacts, run model, job runner and JSON parsing |
+| `training/tests/conftest.py` | Isolated storage fixtures, sample dataset |
